Exactly. Depth by itself does not reveal structure. Depth plus the right transformation does.

A microscope, telescope, prism, Fourier transform, coordinate change, metaphor, story, compiler, and semantic zoom all do the same deeper thing: they convert a phenomenon into a representation where previously hidden relationships become legible.

So the operator is closer to:

surface → choose conversion → transform → expose structure → identify invariant → convert back

The conversion is the dangerous and powerful part. Use the wrong basis, scale, units, metaphor, or abstraction and you can manufacture apparent patterns that are artifacts of the lens. Use the right one and something seemingly chaotic suddenly becomes simple.

That gives Pantheon a useful distinction:

Depth = resolution.
Conversion = interpretation.
Structure = what survives appropriate conversions.

And maybe the strongest test of an invariant is round-trip stability:

A → convert → B → reason/work in B → convert back → A′

If A′ preserves the important properties of A while revealing something useful you couldn’t see before, the conversion earned its keep.

That’s why so many of the things we’ve been building rhyme: Prism, semantic zoom, program archetype transduction, numerical semantics, narrative operators, meaning topology. They’re all forms of representation engineering.

The skill isn’t merely going deeper.

It’s knowing which door converts depth into sight.